  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09124v1</id>
    <title>Toward the Era of Agentic Embodied Intelligence</title>
    <updated>2026-11-06T08:02:15Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09124v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.09124v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Foundation models increasingly guide robot actions, yet sustained autonomy requires efficient control and failure recovery. We introduce A2-MEAS, an embodied multi-agent framework built around code as a scaling interface. Learned correction codes combine corrective language, spatial evidence, execution history, and action proposals. MetaBrain provides training supervision and runtime guidance. A correction encoder and adapter absorb this knowledge into policy weights during training, while fresh codes adapt behavior with frozen weights at deployment. The action model, A2-WLA, adds motion-aware history and a latent world model to a vision-language-action policy. Surprisingly, this simple design with action pretraining and task specialization reaches 0.5755 Q-score on the 50-task BEHAVIOR 2026 public subset before online correction, 2.21x the 2025 winner's reported 0.2605 across different benchmark editions and training configurations. On all 100 BEHAVIOR 2026 public tasks, A2-MEAS achieves 0.6411 Q-score and 45.40% task success. On RoboTwin 2.0, A2-WLA achieves 93.98% average success, rising to 95.05% with the complete system. Across 12 real-robot tasks in five household settings, A2-WLA achieves 57.92% average success versus 26.67% for pi0.5. In long-horizon pour-over coffee preparation, a history-conditioned adapter raises post-failure completion at two filter-handling steps from 14% to 94% and from 8% to 82%, compared with A2-WLA retries. Our training infrastructure delivers 427.1 samples per second, 4.03x the throughput of a pi0.5 reference implementation on matched hardware. We will release the system, models, training scripts, and infrastructure to support efficient embodied learning.</summary>
    <category term="cs.RO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-06T08:02:15Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.RO"/>
    <author>
      <name>Chao Liang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ke Chen</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Fengxiaoxiao Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Shibo Huang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yufeng Sun</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zhaokai Yin</name>
    </author>
