  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08240v1</id>
    <title>FewStepRL: Fine-tuning Few-step Diffusion Models with Reinforcement Learning</title>
    <updated>2026-11-05T16:19:52Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08240v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.08240v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Reinforcement learning (RL) has become a standard post-training method for diffusion models, but most RL objectives roll out the current policy at every iteration, and with a many-step sampler these rollouts typically dominate the cost of training. Few-step models cut this cost, yet they model an average velocity over an interval, whereas existing RL objectives are built for an instantaneous velocity with a many-step sampler. We adapt RL objectives to few-step models systematically in three phases, namely a transformation, a decomposition and an adaptation. The transformation to $x$-space expresses each objective we consider through a common policy prediction, at the clean end by default and at an explicitly marked level for transition or flow-map objectives, so that objectives stated over densities, velocities or rewards become comparable. The decomposition writes the gradient of each of these objectives as the sum of a self-regression toward a reference and a reward augmentation, so that a method reduces, at the gradient level, to a choice of anchors and weights. The adaptation computes each anchor from a few-step model at the end of its transport or along its tangent, with the instantaneous velocity recovered from the network itself or from an auxiliary network. This yields few-step versions of FlowGRPO, AWM, DiffusionNFT, DiffusionOPSD and ReFL in one modular codebase. In our experiments, reward-gradient-based methods are the most effective when the reward is differentiable and its gradient is affordable; otherwise DiffusionNFT-style methods generally reach high rewards in relatively few iterations despite a sometimes higher per-iteration cost. Extra inference steps show no systematic aggregate benefit in our experiments, although selected outputs visibly improve with two to four times as many sampling steps as in training.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-05T16:19:52Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>86pages, 12 figures, technical report</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Haitao Lin</name>
    </author>
    <author>
