  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10304v1</id>
    <title>Longtail-SWE: High-Quality SWE Tasks for Underrepresented Languages</title>
    <updated>2026-11-06T23:04:32Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10304v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10304v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Challenging repository-level benchmarks remain limited for many programming language ecosystems, where filtering scarce candidates with ambiguous descriptions or defective tests can discard valuable engineering problems. We introduce Longtail-SWE, a benchmark of 202 issue-resolution tasks across 13 languages with limited coverage in existing repository-level benchmarks: Clojure, Dart, Elixir, Erlang, F#, Haskell, Julia, Kotlin, Lua, OCaml, R, Scala, and Swift. Its construction uses a solver--judge--repairer pipeline: a solver attempts each candidate task, and a judge reviews the task together with the solver's trajectory and test results to guide revisions to tests and task descriptions. These revisions aim to correct tests that reject valid alternative implementations or accept incorrect solutions while retaining challenging tasks and preserving their intended requirements. We evaluate nine model configurations, including frontier models, and analyze task-solving performance, consistency across repeated attempts, and resource usage. We release all benchmark instances, executable environments, complete construction and evaluation tooling, and agent trajectories, together with an additional set of easier tasks to support evaluation of smaller models.</summary>
    <category term="cs.SE" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-06T23:04:32Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.SE"/>
    <author>
      <name>Vladimir Dobrovolskii</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ibragim Badertdinov</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Alexander Golubev</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11344v1</id>
    <title>Spatial Harness: Grounding Spatial Reasoning in Executable World States with Physics Engines</title>
    <updated>2026-11-08T12:43:14Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11344v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.11344v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Spatial reasoning requires understanding how objects and observers are arranged in a scene and how their relations change with viewpoint or movement. Vision-language models (VLMs) typically perform both steps through model-internal reasoning, forming an implicit scene interpretation from partial views and imagining the consequences of spatial transformations. A misread scene can lead to a wrong answer, while even a correct interpretation does not ensure that imagined transformations satisfy spatial constraints. We introduce Spatial Harness, which represents scenes as explicit and executable world states to ground spatial reasoning in physics-engine feedback. A Perceiver integrates scene constraints extracted from the question with specialized visual estimates to place objects and cameras in a shared coordinate frame. The resulting state is instantiated in a physics engine, where a Reasoner executes viewpoint changes and uses computed measurements and rendered views to guide reasoning. We evaluate on MindCube, SAT, MMSI-Bench, and SpatialRGPT-Bench with open- and closed-weight VLMs. On MindCube, Spatial Harness improves Qwen3.5-27B from 52.70% to 71.14%, surpassing pySpatial by 7.52 percentage points. Given identical annotated object and camera poses, it reaches 98.25% versus 71.81% for model-internal reasoning. Code is publicly available at: https://github.com/tmlr-group/Spatial-Harness</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-08T12:43:14Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>NeurIPS 2026</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Chentao Cao</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zhanke Zhou</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Sangni Duan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Bo Han</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Hang Li</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12875v1</id>
    <title>Click3R: Interactive Stereo 3D Reconstruction with Sparse Correspondence Clicks</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T18:58:02Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12875v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12875v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Despite impressive progress, existing feed-forward large 3D reconstruction models produce a single, irrevocable prediction. When these models fail, there is no mechanism to correct their mistakes. To address this limitation, we introduce Click3R, a framework for interactive geometric correction of feed-forward models using sparse human correspondence clicks. Given an image pair and a small set of cross-view point correspondences provided by a user, Click3R injects geometric constraints into the 3D reconstruction networks through a lightweight point correspondence adapter, enabling the model to resolve ambiguities in challenging scenarios such as repetitive textures, symmetric structures, large viewpoint changes, or textureless regions. To evaluate interactive reconstruction, we curate a challenging benchmark specifically designed to expose failure modes of existing feed-forward 3D reconstruction methods. Experiments show that Click3R reduces reconstruction error with as few as a single correspondence click, improving performance on both standard and our curated benchmarks. Our results highlight that sparse human interaction provides an effective and practical mechanism for correcting geometric reconstruction errors.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T18:58:02Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>NeurIPS 2026. Project page: https://uva-computer-vision-lab.github.io/click3r/</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Hao Gu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jin Yao</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zezhou Cheng</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12041v1</id>
    <title>SMO: Compiling Natural Language Skills into Graphs of Reusable State-Move-Outcome Bricks</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T08:19:04Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12041v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12041v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Large language model agents increasingly rely on reusable natural-language skills, yet growing skill collections remain accumulations of isolated text artifacts that are difficult to compare, deduplicate, and compose. We argue that the reusable content of a skill lies not in its surface wording but in its procedural transitions: which operation to execute under a given state and which observable outcome to produce. Based on this observation, we propose State-Move-Outcome (SMO), a compilation-like framework that decomposes raw skills into reusable procedural bricks and organizes admitted bricks as an externalized symbolic structure. When compiling a skill, SMO first retrieves and composes existing bricks, dynamically connects them through Outcome-to-State conditions, and creates a candidate brick only for an uncovered procedure; the candidate becomes reusable only after validation and admission. Experiments on SkillsBench v1.1 demonstrate that SMO represents 232 rawSkills from 87 underlying tasks with 95 shared bricks, reducing the corresponding skill representations from 366,472 to 149,154 tokens--a $\mathbf{59.3}\times$ reduction and $\mathbf{2.46}\times$ compression. Across 232 skill-conditioned evaluation instances, SMO raises the average task score from $\mathbf{0.6379}$ to $\mathbf{0.7478}$ and increases 5/5 Pass instances from $\mathbf{138}$ to $\mathbf{164}$, while matching or outperforming rawSkill on $\mathbf{208}$ of $\mathbf{232}$ instances. It also reduces average runtime token consumption by $\mathbf{38.38\%}$. These results show that compiling natural-language skills into shared procedural units can jointly improve skill reuse, representation efficiency, and downstream execution.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T08:19:04Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Chao Chen</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jiashuo Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yunhang Shen</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Liangyu Lian</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Rongrong Ji</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08264v2</id>
    <title>SplatAnything: Gaussian Spacing Normalization for Feed-forward 3DGS across Arbitrary Scenes</title>
    <updated>2026-11-08T08:04:42Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08264v2" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.08264v2" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We identify a key limitation of existing feed-forward 3DGS methods: Gaussian attributes are typically predicted in a radial-distance-normalized space that canonicalizes a global radial statistic of the reconstructed geometry, while the spacing between neighboring Gaussian centers can still vary substantially across scene scales and camera configurations. This mismatch induces systematic shifts in Gaussian-scale targets, leading to ambiguous supervision. To address this, we propose SplatAnything with Gaussian Spacing Normalization (GSNorm), which canonicalizes a robust scene-level statistic of Gaussian spacing to provide a more consistent spatial reference for Gaussian prediction. To rigorously assess our method, we further establish a systematic benchmark spanning diverse datasets, input sparsity levels, and challenging extrapolated viewpoints. Extensive experiments demonstrate that SplatAnything achieves state-of-the-art performance, with particularly strong gains under substantial variations in scene scale and viewpoint. Project page: https://zju3dv.github.io/SplatAnything</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-05T16:37:29Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Hao Yu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zhenwei Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yongzhen Hu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yuning Gong</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Junta Wu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xiaowei Zhou</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Hujun Bao</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Tengfei Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Chunchao Guo</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Sida Peng</name>
    </author>
