  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05791v1</id>
    <title>FlyHash for LLM Agent Memory: An Empirical Validation and a New Application</title>
    <updated>2026-11-04T00:57:24Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05791v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.05791v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Large language model (LLM) agents increasingly rely on external memory stores to retain information across conversations, typically via dense vector embeddings searched with approximate nearest-neighbor methods. The fruit fly's olfactory circuit computes a locality-sensitive hash of sensory input using a strikingly different mechanism - sparse random projection followed by winner-take-all competition - and prior work (Dasgupta et al., 2017) showed this scheme, dubbed FlyHash, preserves neighbor structure better per bit than conventional random-hyperplane hashing (SimHash) on several benchmarks. We apply FlyHash, to our knowledge for the first time, to LLM agent memory retrieval, and ask three questions a practitioner actually needs answered: does the quality advantage hold under a correctly matched-bit protocol (it does, robustly, across 10 seeds and four datasets spanning two modalities, with one specific, previously undocumented comparison pitfall identified and corrected along the way); does that advantage translate into real memory and latency savings, including against the dense-vector indexing (HNSW) practitioners default to today (no: at equal bits FlyHash's codes are larger and slower to compare than SimHash's, and HNSW dominates both hash methods at ordinary scale; a sparse-set comparison we introduce recovers most but not all of a naive latency gap, leaving hashing's practical case narrower and more specific than the quality result alone implies); and does the advantage survive genuine, non-circular agent-memory relevance labels rather than the nearest-neighbor-by-embedding proxy this literature otherwise relies on (it does). We report all three answers precisely, including the two that narrow rather than flatter the headline result, and release our implementation, a working agent-memory demo, and a full account of the methodological pitfalls we found and corrected.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-04T00:57:24Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>19 pages. Code, data and interactive demo: https://github.com/mohanji/flyhash-agent-memory (archived at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23903302)</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Mohan Bheemadolu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Chrisogonas O. Odhiambo</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01729v1</id>
    <title>ODE Policy: Robot Learning via Action Dynamics, Not Actions</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T09:25:12Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01729v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.01729v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Most robot policies, whether trained by imitation learning (IL) or reinforcement learning (RL), map observations to action values at discrete control steps and leave the continuity of physical motion to be learned implicitly. We propose ODE policy, which learns the dynamics of the action rather than the action itself. A neural ordinary differential equation (ODE) learns the vector field of the action by matching the integrated actions to the targets over short action chunks in IL and single control steps in RL. A lightweight diversity head captures multi-modal behavior by randomizing the direction of the field. At deployment, each action is integrated from the previous one, so the executed trajectory is continuous by construction. We evaluate it as a visuomotor policy, as the action head of vision-language-action (VLA) and world action models (WAMs), and as the actor of model-based RL against 14 baselines on unimanual, bimanual, dexterous, locomotion, and loco-manipulation tasks. In IL, against flow matching, it reaches 5.9$\times$ the success, 5.4$\times$ the generalization, and 3.9$\times$ the mode balance after one epoch, and generates actions 10.8$\times$ faster. It mostly moves more smoothly than human demonstrations. On 14 real-world tasks across single-arm, dual-arm, dexterous, and humanoid robots, it achieves the highest success rate. In RL, it further speeds up SuperMimic for humanoid motion tracking and smooths its actions. Efficient in learning, data, parameters, and execution, ODE policy offers a promising path to scaling robot learning.</summary>
    <category term="cs.RO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T09:25:12Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>35 pages, 23 figures</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.RO"/>
    <author>
      <name>Jindou Jia</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jingliang Li</name>
    </author>
