  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11740v1</id>
    <title>Creative Priming: Decoupling Process from Product to Escape the Creative Paradox</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T01:28:05Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11740v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.11740v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Many of the most consequential advances promised by AI, from curing diseases and inventing new technologies to formulating new scientific theories, are fundamentally creative acts. Yet creativity is uniquely difficult to learn because it faces a creative paradox: learning to repeat past examples of creative success makes those specific examples more likely to recur, inhibiting the very creativity desired. This work argues that creativity is better modeled as an explicit process rather than as a property of individual artifacts, and proposes to learn the underlying generative principles behind the creative process. In particular, to escape the paradox, we introduce creative priming (CP), wherein a trained priming model generates a prompt-specific primer that assists a response model in being creative, explicitly decoupling the creative process from response generation. The priming model learns through GRPO, rewarding primers that lead to responses that are novel, high-quality, and diverse, thereby reinforcing the process that yields creative responses without reinforcing any specific response.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T01:28:05Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Alon Albalak</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Sebastian Gabriel</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>David B. D'Ambrosio</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Joel Lehman</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Kenneth O. Stanley</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12762v1</id>
    <title>Sage-Taiyi: A Metacognitive Architecture for Scientific Discovery</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T17:38:05Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12762v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12762v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We introduce Sage-Taiyi, a metacognition-inspired architecture that uses accumulated scientific understanding to guide what to investigate next and how to investigate it. Through explicit monitoring and control, the system revises its understanding of evidence, assumptions, and unresolved questions, then uses these assessments to adapt research targets and select reusable reasoning methods. On Taiyi-Bench, an AI co-scientist ideation benchmark comprising 265 tasks from 50 papers across ten disciplines, Sage-Taiyi ranks first on all eight metrics of historical rediscovery and open-ended ideation, achieving an Overall score of 54.0 compared with 47.9 for the strongest baseline, AI Scientist-v2. It also performs well on two external scientific ideation benchmarks. Five expert-guided collaborations further connect its proposals to experimental and computational validation across chemistry, materials science, and engineering. In chemistry, reaction-condition optimization raises the yield of cobalt-catalyzed carbonylation from trace amounts to 84% and photocatalytic allylic amination from 13% to 69%. These results support metacognitive regulation as an approach to scientific ideation and its progression toward practical validation.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T17:38:05Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>38 pages, 18 figures. Project homepage: https://alpha-innovator.github.io/Sage-Taiyi/</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
