  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06808v1</id>
    <title>LLMs Don't Know What They Know: Benchmarking Temporal Self-Knowledge for Financial Backtesting</title>
    <updated>2026-11-04T17:24:02Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06808v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.06808v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Financial backtests of large language models (LLMs) are meaningful only if the model did not already know the outcomes it is asked to predict. Practitioners guard against this by trusting a vendor's stated knowledge cutoff, by asking the model what it knows, or by instructing it to answer as of a past date. To verify whether any of these safeguards can be trusted, we introduce FinDendro, a benchmark of 2,226 dated U.S. facts from 1999 to 2026, covering monthly macroeconomic series, month-end stock prices and Federal Reserve decisions. Each fact yields four prompts: a value question, Recall and Predict questions that pair each candidate value with a claim of knowing or guessing without a declared date, and a Predict question with a historical declared date. Controls for persistence and dominant outcomes keep chance accuracy near 0.27. Results show that for most frontier models, self-reports understate knowledge. GPT-5 answers 76% of controlled facts correctly but claims to know 38%, and answers 61% of the facts it says it does not know. Knowledge claims can persist beyond the measured knowledge boundary, by up to seven quarters, and a declared date removes most of the claims of prior knowledge while leaving accuracy unchanged. Backtests inside a model's training window therefore cannot be certified leak-free by self-report, instruction or stated cutoff. These findings motivate behavioral measurement of temporal knowledge boundaries, and FinDendro provides a benchmark and procedure for selecting backtest periods based on demonstrated factual knowledge.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-04T17:24:02Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>20 pages, 4 figures, 11 tables</arxiv:comment>
