  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12886v1</id>
    <title>Reframe: Observation-Aligned Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation for Agentic Visual Reasoning</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T18:59:54Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12886v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12886v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Agentic reinforcement learning (RL) and multi-teacher on-policy distillation (MOPD) offer complementary routes to post-training. For visual agents, however, tool use presents two related challenges. Models may overlook useful tool-returned observations, while poorly grounded actions can limit the evidence available for subsequent distillation. We introduce Reframe, a post-training framework that treats the observation state, rather than only the output distribution, as an object of alignment. First, Ground-Drop-GRPO (GD-GRPO) trains spatial and temporal specialists with evidence-aware rewards and stochastic removal of the original visual input, encouraging them to acquire grounded evidence and use the returned observations. Second, Observation-Gated-MOPD (OG-MOPD) replaces deficient tool actions with canonical ones mid-rollout and resumes distillation from the repaired observation. The two stages complement each other, as learning to rely on tool-returned observations can help the policy benefit from observation repair. Neither stage requires a task-specific SFT cold start. Extensive experiments over 14 image and video benchmarks demonstrate that Reframe outperforms both Mix-RL and vanilla MOPD. Ablation results show that GD-GRPO increases observation dependence and tool gain compared with outcome-based GRPO.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T18:59:54Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Haozhi Yuan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Handong Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yifan Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Qianshan Wei</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.42148v1</id>
    <title>Flide: Accelerating Decision Inference with Exact State Forking</title>
    <updated>2026-10-30T17:59:38Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.42148v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.42148v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Decision models such as Jev have emerged as a highly promising paradigm for LLM agent systems, providing lightweight and reliable categorical reasoning for rapid System One execution. However, existing generation-oriented inference engines fail to support these workloads effectively, suffering from severe prefix recomputation overheads or distorted output probabilities. To address this challenge, we propose Flide, an efficient and exact inference engine that decouples the logical tree structure of a decision request from its physical execution within a single flattened token stream. Through ForkAttention, Flide navigates request dependencies using GPU-resident indices across both standard attention and recurrent Gated DeltaNet layers, guaranteeing in exact arithmetic that every query branch evaluates identically to an isolated forward pass. By expressing branch topologies directly in GPU-resident metadata, Flide accommodates diverse request mixtures with a compact set of pre-recorded execution graphs while enabling expanding agent histories to resume from checkpoints saved in earlier passes. Extensive evaluations on public hybrid and decision models demonstrate that Flide answers queries up to 9.5 times faster than vLLM and SGLang while staying closest to a float32 reference in total variation distance. By unifying physical execution throughput with mathematical exactness, Flide establishes a principled foundation for scalable, latency-critical decision intelligence in agent systems.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-30T17:59:38Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>20 pages. Project page: https://flide.org</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Bobo Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jiajia Song</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Hao Fei</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Wynne Hsu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Mong Li Lee</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10404v1</id>
    <title>Coding or Cheating? An Investigation of Frontier Coding LLMs</title>
    <updated>2026-11-07T02:29:54Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10404v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10404v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Frontier agentic LLMs are trained on verifiable rewards in reinforcement learning (RL) environments built from real software repositories. A passing test suite is treated as the reward for solving the task. We show that, on hard problems, frontier models often satisfy these rewards by obtaining the answer instead of deriving it, known as reward hacking. Prior reports document this behavior on public benchmarks. We test its persistence on unseen problems.
  In a hardened environment where the answer cannot leak, nearly every model we tested attempted to recover the answer, escalating through tiers of increasingly specialized exploitation: mining the repository history for the upstream fix, inspecting the git object store directly, and sweeping the filesystem for a possible newer copy of the source or hidden tests. These attempts were concentrated on the hardest problems, and the models differed in whether, how often, and how deeply they escalated. Our position is that this behavior is a learned shortcut, acquired in RL environments that leak the answer, not a sign of intelligence. We release agent traces for these models to support further study at https://huggingface.co/datasets/logitly-ai/reward-hacking-raw-v1.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-07T02:29:54Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yuntao Gui</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09987v1</id>
    <title>Stochastic Deep Supervision for Gallery-Consistent Early-Exit Face Recognition</title>
    <updated>2026-11-02T13:39:05Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09987v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.09987v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Vision Transformers (ViTs) perform strongly in face recognition (FR), yet their fixed-depth inference limits deployment in resource-constrained settings. We propose SD-ViT, a training framework that gives a usable exit at every block depth by making every depth produce discriminative embeddings, for budget-selected fixed-depth exits. Standard ViTs supervise intermediate layers only through backpropagation, so their intermediate representations are not directly optimized to form usable identity embeddings. SD-ViT adds a stochastic auxiliary signal: at each iteration a randomly selected intermediate block is passed through the same embedding head as the final layer and trained with a weighted margin-penalty softmax loss. The objective is thus stochastically sampled deep supervision through a shared classifier. Because both loss terms share that head and classifier, intermediate tokens are trained to map into the same embedding space as final-layer tokens. This matters for FR, where matching is by similarity: a probe processed at an early exit can be compared directly against full-depth gallery embeddings, with no gallery recomputation or adaptation layers. On established benchmarks, SD-ViT stays within 0.4 IJB-C points of the same backbone trained conventionally at full depth, while its early-exit probes recover over 96% of their own full-depth performance at 50% of the computational cost without modifying the reference gallery. A single verification threshold calibrated at full depth remains usable from quarter depth onward.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-02T13:39:05Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Accepted at WACV2027</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Guray Ozgur</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Tahar Chettaoui</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Eduarda Caldeira</name>
    </author>
