  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01362v1</id>
    <title>"PRISM" Is Not All You Need: Cross-Topic Name Collisions and Scientific Paper Retrieval</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:08Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01362v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.01362v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Scientific names increasingly connect papers from different research topics, leaving readers to recover a paper's identity from context. We study this convergence in 564,861 AI-related arXiv records. Cross-topic name collision grows 4.33-fold from a pooled 2020-2022 reference to 2026, compared with 1.12-fold within topics. A second sentence encoder reproduces the pattern, and name-group resampling quantifies its uncertainty. Fixed word-frequency bands locate the main increase in an intermediate-frequency vocabulary shared by many research areas. We then measure the ambiguity that remains after supplying research clues. With the same three-term query construction, shared-name targets retain matched identification deficits of 25.7 percentage points under title-based BM25 and 6.9 points under title-plus-abstract E5. Within fixed same-name candidates, listwise language-model ranking reaches 89.2% using the query and candidate titles. Topic-specific comparisons show that context resolves more cross-topic ambiguity. The evaluation separates candidate competition, document fields, and contextual ranking through lexical, dense, and language-model baselines. Source-linked cases and a portable evaluation collection make the phenomenon inspectable and reusable for scientific retrieval and research assistants.</summary>
    <category term="cs.IR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.DL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T00:00:08Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.IR"/>
    <author>
      <name>Jingjie Ning</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xueqi Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yibo Kong</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01182v1</id>
    <title>Fast and Exact Relative-Position Attention via Structure-Aware Kernels</title>
    <updated>2026-10-31T18:29:06Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01182v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.01182v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Relative-position attention mechanisms offer compelling alternatives to RoPE across various transformer architectures, delivering superior performance in domains such as audio. These mechanisms typically modify attention scores by adding content-position or bias-position terms. Unlike RoPE, which encodes position via per-token rotations directly on the queries and keys, relative-position terms generally require pairwise offset-dependent computation or lookups, complicating their integration into fused attention kernels and introducing severe memory, bandwidth, and compute overheads as sequences scale. To address this bottleneck, we propose an exact attention algorithm that incorporates additive relative-position terms into FlashAttention-style tiled computation. Our method (1) uses offset-aware tiling to avoid redundant indexing and memory accesses, (2) computes only the necessary key/query-relation products and reuses them across token pairs in both forward and backward passes, and (3) uses offset-based gradient sharding and aggregation, eliminating atomic contention. We provide a unified, efficient Triton implementation across diverse relative-position attention mechanisms and evaluate it against naive and specialized implementations. For 8K-token sequences, our method speeds up the forward (backward) pass by 1.8-6.4$\times$ (2.0-21$\times$) and reduces peak memory up to 2.9-128$\times$ (1.4-54$\times$) over the strongest baseline for each mechanism, enabling long-context end-to-end transcription that is 1.8$\times$ faster and uses 2.4$\times$ less memory. Our method thus provides a drop-in, hardware-efficient solution for scaling relative-position attention transformers.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.DC" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-31T18:29:06Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>25 pages, 2 figures, 14 tables</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Delyan Boychev</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ivo Petrov</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Dimitar I. Dimitrov</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08452v1</id>
    <title>ReproBridge: Building Reproducibility into ML Research Artifacts</title>
    <updated>2026-11-05T18:45:36Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08452v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.08452v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Reproducing machine learning experiments requires reconstructing procedures from information scattered across papers, released code, and supporting artifacts. For coding agents, reproduction success can therefore depend on their ability to fill in missing details, rather than on the released artifacts alone. We ask whether preserving recovered procedures and decisions improves subsequent independent reproduction. We define a standardized artifact design comprising a refined codebase, environment setup, experiment launchers, and a reproduction guide, and introduce ReproBridge, an agent workflow for constructing these refined reproducible artifacts. ReproBridge-Bench provides 1,029 tasks from 387 experiments across 36 papers, anchored by five human-verified reference scores per task. Agents attempt tasks in fresh sessions with raw code or refined artifacts; a separate Auditor checks execution evidence. Across four evaluated models, refined artifacts raise mean reproduction success from 76.4% to 92.6% and reduce downstream API spending by 19.7% on average. The cross-model success-rate spread narrows from 21.6 to 3.9 percentage points, with all four models achieving mean success rates above 90%. Removing any component reduces success in the ablation study, with the largest losses from code and environment removal. These results suggest that preserving execution knowledge helps even models with lower raw-code success reproduce experiments more reliably and at lower API cost.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-05T18:45:36Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yubo Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Rui Meng</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jaehyun Nam</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Tomas Pfister</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jinsung Yoon</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04720v1</id>
    <title>OneTrans-GSR: A Generative Slate Ranking Paradigm Unifying Reranking and Mix-Ranking with One Autoregressive Transformer</title>
    <updated>2026-11-03T11:42:02Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04720v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.04720v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Industrial recommender systems allocate substantial computation to pointwise candidate scoring, yet separate reranking and mix-ranking stages still assemble the final exposure slate using hand-crafted rules and specialized low-capacity models. Mix-ranking combines product cards, short videos, livestreams, and advertising cards supplied by distinct upstream business channels. Although these rules and models encode valuable domain knowledge, they are inherently limited in leveraging additional data and computation. They constrain the combinatorial space of candidate slates and determine objective trade-offs through manual tuning. To enable scalable and effective slate generation, we reformulate reranking and mix-ranking as a unified learning problem whose performance can improve with computation. Specifically, we instantiate this formulation across the reranking and mix-ranking stages and propose OneTrans-GSR, a single decoder-only policy that extends our deployed OneTrans backbone to generate the exposure slate end-to-end. During training, a reward model evaluates complete slates and guides reinforcement learning toward slate-level and long-term objectives. During decoding, business constraints are explicitly enforced. Offline evaluation shows that slate quality consistently improves as reward-model and policy compute increase. Deployed in a large-scale industrial recommendation system, OneTrans-GSR mixes heterogeneous content types within a unified slate. In online A/B tests, it improves GMV/u by 2.63%, advertiser value by 2.06%, and repurchase rate by 2.03%, while reducing end-to-end latency across the reranking and mix-ranking stages by 13%. These results mark a paradigm shift in slate generation: moving beyond human-engineered heuristics toward a general learned policy creates a scalable path for further algorithmic optimization and compute-driven improvement.</summary>
    <category term="cs.IR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-03T11:42:02Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>26 pages, 6 figures, 11 tables</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.IR"/>
    <author>
      <name>Feng Cheng</name>
    </author>
