  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04434v1</id>
    <title>Sketchpad: certified exact arithmetic with a small vision-language model</title>
    <updated>2026-11-03T07:25:42Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04434v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.04434v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Language models are accurate on most long-arithmetic problems, but their accuracy is estimated on samples and carries no guarantee for an unseen input; tool use is exact but moves the computation outside the network. We show that exactness at all input lengths becomes a finite, checkable property of a network when each of its steps reads only a bounded view of an external working memory. In Sketchpad, Qwen3.5-0.8B with a low-rank adapter executes arithmetic procedures on a rendered sheet. Each forward pass reads a three-cell view and emits one step: a digit, a carry or borrow, and the next operation. A fixed runtime moves the view and writes no digit of a result. We prove in Lean that the model is then exact on all inputs of any length if and only if it is exact on the finite set of views the runtime presents, that no finite test suffices when steps also read earlier steps, and that the guarantee tolerates bounded numerical perturbation and extends to compositions of procedures. All 2,446 views pass on the deployment device, a laptop GPU running the model in a web browser. The certified model performs signed addition and subtraction, long multiplication and division, square roots and greatest common divisors at arbitrary length. It solved 200 of 200 pre-registered problems in the browser, and 220 of 220 sums of up to 1,000 digits and products of up to 20 digits, of which GPT-6.1 Sol, the strongest of ten other models, solved 217. A symbolic runtime built on these procedures solves, expands and factors polynomials with every number computed by the model, and a multiplicative gate on the adapter recovers the base model exactly when the sheet is not in use.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-03T07:25:42Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>25 pages, 7 figures (6 + 1 Extended Data), 6 Extended Data tables. Demo: https://neovand.github.io/sketchpad/demo ; code (Apache 2.0): https://github.com/NeoVand/sketchpad ; model: https://huggingface.co/mohsenvand/sketchpad-0.8b-preview</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Mostafa 'Neo' Mohsenvand</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06844v1</id>
    <title>Where to Connect? Boosting MLLMs via Dynamic Gated Pathways across ALL ViT and LLM Layers</title>
    <updated>2026-11-04T18:01:24Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06844v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.06844v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>A central yet under-explored question in Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) is where to connect---how visual information from a hierarchical vision encoder should be wired into the layer-wise semantics of a large language model. Mainstream MLLMs inject a fixed single-layer visual representation into the LLM and treat all LLM layers as a single consumer, ignoring that different layers demand different visual granularities. Recent multi-layer fusion methods either compress hierarchical features on the encoder side or rely on predefined sparse or hierarchical layer-to-layer connections, and therefore stop short of modeling adaptive cross-layer interactions between the ViT and LLM hierarchies. We address this gap with DGP (Dynamic Gated Pathways), a routing mechanism that establishes all-to-all connections between every ViT layer and every LLM layer, with each LLM layer dynamically gating its preference over visual layers conditioned on its own semantic state. Built on top of LLaVA-1.5-7B and 13B, DGP delivers consistent gains over the LLaVA-1.5-7B baseline (e.g., +4.2 on SQA$^{I}$, +3.3 on MM-Vet, +3.1 on VizWiz and LLaVA$^{W}$) and surpasses prior multi-layer visual fusion methods on most benchmarks. Layer-wise routing analyses further show that different LLM layers do select different visual granularities on demand, supporting both the effectiveness and interpretability of the proposed dynamic gated pathways. The code is available: https://github.com/YingyingYan1/DGP.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-04T18:01:24Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>24 pages (10 pages main text + references + appendix), 15 figures. Accepted at the 40th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2026)</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yingying Yan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jiaqi Tang</name>
    </author>
