  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07025v1</id>
    <title>LowRAM: Low-Rank Keys and Amortised Values for Composable KV Cache Compression at Test-Time</title>
    <updated>2026-11-04T19:00:06Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07025v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.07025v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Autoregressive decoding relies on the KV cache, whose memory footprint grows linearly with context length and caps batch size and throughput. Existing post-training methods exploit redundancy across tokens, layers, and feature dimensions, or lower numerical precision. We identify a complementary axis: within each attention head, values are largely predictable from their corresponding keys. We exploit this with value cache amortisation, which fits a lightweight per-head predictor at test time and stores residuals only for the tokens it reconstructs worst, giving $4\times$ near-lossless value compression. The predictor can be initialised by meta-learning for substantially reduced prefill overhead, or fitted from scratch in settings that allow a one-off cost, such as multi-query inference. We pair value amortisation with cross-layer low-rank key compression to produce LowRAM, an asymmetric compression pipeline that stays close to lossless across three model families. By reconstructing only a selected subset of KV pairs at each decoding step, LowRAM can keep decoding overhead minimal and achieve up to $5\times$ higher peak decode throughput at $4\times$ compression. LowRAM further composes with quantisation and eviction, reaching $26.9\times$ compression at $94.5\%$ of baseline performance on long contexts. These results show that treating keys and values as structurally distinct enables efficient, aggressive, and composable KV cache compression.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-04T19:00:06Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Teresa Delgado</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Hugo Hazard</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Martin Benfeghoul</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jun Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zafeirios Fountas</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06748v2</id>
    <title>EvoWorld: Towards Out-of-Sight World Evolution in Generative Video Models</title>
    <updated>2026-11-05T04:27:21Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06748v2" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.06748v2" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Generative video models are increasingly used as visual world simulators, yet their primary strength lies in rendering plausible pixels rather than reasoning about how a world should evolve. Coherent world evolution requires more than visual fidelity: when an event occurs, a model must infer its consequences for the entities in the world and then render the resulting state. For example, after a wild beast appears, the model should not only depict the wild beast, but also infer whether a nearby person should flee, take cover, or fight back based on the realized scene. Existing diffusion-based video models lack an explicit mechanism for maintaining world state and reasoning over such event-conditioned consequences, often producing visually plausible but behaviorally static or causally inconsistent outputs. To make this reasoning gap concrete and measurable, we propose a new problem, Out-of-Sight Causal Evolution, which requires a model to render an event, reason about its consequences for the evolving world state, and reveal a causally plausible outcome as the world continues to evolve, even out of sight. As a baseline reference solution, we introduce EvoWorld, a brain-renderer collaboration framework in which a vision-language model serves as the world brain: it observes generated segments, maintains an evolving account of the world, and reasons about plausible state transitions, while a segment-autoregressive video renderer realizes them visually. We further introduce EvoWorld-Data and EvoWorld-Bench for training and evaluating event realization, causal reactions, and revisit consistency. Experiments show that EvoWorld substantially improves event-conditioned causal evolution over recent models while preserving visual quality and consistency, establishing a reference point for reasoning-guided, autonomous world evolution beyond the current field of view.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-04T16:26:45Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yequan Bie</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Dengyang Jiang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zhixuan Chen</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xiaozhuan Liang</name>
    </author>
