  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08969v1</id>
    <title>PhysioWave-T: Token-Efficient Wavelet Foundation Models for Biosignals</title>
    <updated>2026-11-06T04:30:15Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08969v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.08969v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We present PhysioWave-T, a token-efficient wavelet foundation model family for EEG, ECG, and sEMG. Dynamic scale folding recombines J+1 wavelet subbands prior to tokenization, compressing sequence length from (J+1)CS to CS tokens for J levels, C channels, and S temporal patches. Route-specific front ends and learned channel-name embeddings allow one Transformer per modality to handle diverse montages and sampling rates, while unseen setups reuse the backbone via lightweight adapters. We pre-train a 30M-parameter EEG model on over 15M windows (19--128 channels), alongside ECG (30M) and sEMG (12M) encoders. Across four EEG routes, scale folding cuts Transformer latency by 4.0--7.2x and peak memory by 6.8--12.7x over scale expansion without accuracy loss. Our EEG encoder leads 8 of 9 metrics against BENDR, BIOT, LaBraM, and EEGPT, and surpasses LUNA-L on wearable seizure detection with fewer parameters. Reliability-aware latent fusion (RALF) further integrates summary tokens from independently pre-trained encoders via sample-adaptive weights. On SeizeIT2, RALF raises event sensitivity at 11 false alarms/h from 61.4% to 74.4% and window AUPRC from 0.321 to 0.399 over the best unimodal baseline, outperforming weighted-logit and concatenation fusion across all variants. PhysioWave-T also achieves state-of-the-art sEMG accuracies and top macro-F1 on PTB-XL Super and Chapman-Shaoxing. Code and models: https://github.com/ForeverBlue816/PhysioWave.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-06T04:30:15Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>15 pages, 11 figures, 5 tables. Code: https://github.com/ForeverBlue816/PhysioWave , Models &amp; Weights: https://huggingface.co/collections/ForeverBlue/physiowave-t</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yanlong Chen</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Simone Benatti</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Luca Benini</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Cuntai Guan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yawei Li</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09634v1</id>
    <title>Steerable Multi-Objective Group-Relative Preference Optimization with Jointly Trained Low-Rank Adapters</title>
    <updated>2026-11-06T15:50:32Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09634v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.09634v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Language model deployment often requires different trade-offs between competing objectives as user preferences change, yet standard post-training methods typically produce a single policy for a fixed objective weighting. We introduce LambdaMix, a new parameter efficient method for learning a preference conditioned family of policies with group-relative online reinforcement learning. LambdaMix jointly trains one low-rank adapter per objective over a frozen base model. One preference vector selects the adapter mixture that generates responses and weights the separately normalized rewards used to update the adapters. A single training run supports changing preferences at inference without further optimization. We experiment with four models on helpfulness and harmlessness and on solving math problems while following instructions. We also evaluate the two largest models on rewriting comments to preserve wording while reducing clues about personal attributes. On the first two tasks, LambdaMix achieves higher hypervolume than all evaluated conditioned and policy merging baselines across all four models. LambdaMix uses fewer training completions and stores fewer adapter parameters than a set of GRPO specialist policies. With architecture and initialization fixed, varying adapter mixtures and objective weights together gives wider score ranges and higher coverage than varying either alone.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-06T15:50:32Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Preprint. Under review</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Constantinos Karouzos</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Nikolaos Aletras</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2501.08686v2</id>
    <title>Knowledge Graph-based Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Schema Matching</title>
    <updated>2026-11-06T14:56:30Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.08686v2" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.08686v2" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Traditional similarity-based schema matching methods fail to resolve semantic ambiguities and conflicts in complex, domain-specific mappings due to a lack of commonsense and domain-specific knowledge. In parallel, large language models (LLMs) without sufficient background knowledge tend to hallucinate, further complicating schema matching. To overcome these limitations, we introduce KG-RAG4SM, a Knowledge Graph-based Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Schema Matching. This method integrates novel retrieval mechanisms, including LLM-based, vector-based, graph traversal-based, and hybrid techniques with advanced reranking schemes to extract the most relevant subgraphs from large external KGs. Our approach achieves superior results without requiring re-training, as evidenced by improvements of 39.79% in precision and 30.56% in F1 score over LLM-based state-of-the-art (SOTA) methods (e.g., Jellyfish-8B) on the MIMIC dataset, and enhancements of 69.20% in precision and 21.99% in F1 score over pre-trained language model (PLM)-based SOTA methods (e.g., SMAT) on the Synthea dataset. Additionally, KG-RAG4SM scales effectively across KGs from commonsense KGs to domain-specific ones, while its instruction-tuning knowledge exclusion component ensures truthful performance in end-to-end schema matching tasks despite the suboptimal relevance of KG context. Our empirical analysis and case studies from real-world scenarios show that the hallucination problem in LLMs for schema matching is well mitigated by our solution.</summary>
    <category term="cs.DB" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.IR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2025-01-15T09:32:37Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>To appear in ACM TKDD</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.DB"/>
    <author>
      <name>Chuangtao Ma</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Sriom Chakrabarti</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Arijit Khan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Bálint Molnár</name>
    </author>
    <arxiv:doi>10.1145/3862591</arxiv:doi>
