  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11499v1</id>
    <title>JoMoHa: Joint Model--Harness Optimization with Reinforcement Learning</title>
    <updated>2026-11-08T16:58:39Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11499v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.11499v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>The performance of language agents depends jointly on model weights and the harness. Optimizing either alone can be constrained by the other component, limiting overall performance gains. Our analysis of naive alternating training reveals progressive incompatibility between the model and harness, which increasingly interferes with task execution and may also undermine model training, leading to performance degradation. To coordinate the evolution of models and harnesses, we introduce JoMoHa, to our knowledge the first framework to jointly train an executable harness generation policy and a task execution policy through reinforcement learning. JoMoHa outperforms the evaluated weight-only and harness-only baselines and achieves the highest success rates among the evaluated methods on ALFWorld, WebShop, and DBBench, averaging 70.3% and reaching 97.0% on ALFWorld. By learning both task execution and runtime improvement from its own generated trajectories, JoMoHa performs recursive self-improvement (RSI).</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-08T16:58:39Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>10 pages</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Xudong Wu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Guangchen Lan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Mingqi Yuan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jiayu Chen</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08175v2</id>
    <title>PCBRouteAgent: How Close Are LLM Agents to Becoming PCB Routing Engineers?</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T16:23:03Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08175v2" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.08175v2" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Automating printed circuit board (PCB) routing requires coordinating board-level connectivity decisions with geometric execution under strict design rules. Although recent agents can interact with native routing tools, reliable routing on complex boards remains challenging. We present PCBRouteAgent, a multimodal PCB routing agent inspired by how human engineers coordinate global strategy with local execution. A Global Routing Planner combines board images and structured electrical and geometric information to specify connection topology, routing order, corridors, and layer transitions. A Detailed Routing Executor realizes these guides through KiCad's native routing engine and performs bounded local recovery. Unresolved failures are returned as structured feedback, enabling targeted guide revisions while preserving accepted routes. We evaluate PCBRouteAgent on 247 synthetic and real-world boards from four PCBWorld-Bench test splits. It achieves clean-pass rates of 100%, 94.9%, 100%, and 40% on D2, D3-A, D3-B, and D3-C, respectively. On the three real-world subsets, PCBRouteAgent outperforms the best evaluated baseline in clean-pass rate by 13.9, 36, and 10 percentage points, respectively.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-05T15:31:15Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yue Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jilei Sun</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Liqiang Jing</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yijie Wei</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ruohan Yang</name>
    </author>
