  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12552v1</id>
    <title>SciMARS: Multi-Agent Recursive Self-Improvement for Closed-Loop Scientific Discovery</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T15:20:32Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12552v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12552v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We propose SciMARS, a framework for recursive task-local adaptation of multi-agent harnesses in closed-loop scientific discovery. Multi-agent systems coordinate analysis, experimental design, and verification as scientific evidence evolves. However, two problems limit their effectiveness: (i) without shared memory, agents lose observations and repeat work; (ii) fixed role procedures, handoffs, and memory policies perpetuate mistakes despite retained evidence. We address these problems with a shared evidence graph and separate component editors. SciMARS adapts roles and coordination; SciMARS + Memory adds a third editor for how agents record, retrieve, and receive evidence. Candidate probes guide composition and selection of the next harness. We evaluate scientific discovery through experimental-data replay and a digital twin. SciMARS + Memory resolves 12/18 GPT-6-Astra and 2/18 GLM-5.3 attempts on Autonomous Science Bench, compared with 8/18 and 1/18 for the base multi-agent team. On ScienceAgentBench and AppWorld, it solves 14/38 and 11/20 tasks, compared with 11/38 and 5/20 for the base team. These results support combining shared memory with harness adaptation across scientific and interactive tasks.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T15:20:32Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Weimin Wu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Han Liu</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36896v1</id>
    <title>PREEN: Exemplar-Based Color Style Transfer with Smartphone Editor Presets</title>
    <updated>2026-10-27T09:30:35Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36896v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.36896v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We present PREEN (Preset Regression via an Exemplar-based Editor Network), which casts color style transfer as regression onto the 29 parameters of a deterministic image editor. Given a content image and a style reference, PREEN predicts a preset, a vector of editor slider values that is interpretable and editor-consumable, not a raster image. The key obstacle is supervision, since an arbitrary real reference lacks a preset ground truth. We therefore supervise with a style-transfer teacher, whose target looks we invert into the editor's parameter space as pseudo-presets, then pretrain a CLIP-conditioned regressor and refine it with LoRA under an image-level CIELAB loss. On the human-curated PST50 benchmark, PREEN applies the reference's color while preserving structural fidelity, leading the baselines on pixel- and perceptual-fidelity metrics. The preset is compact and predicted at sub-second on-device latency. The low-dimensional subspace also exposes a single inference-time strength knob that traces a continuous default-to-vivid trajectory in one forward pass, with every intermediate state a valid editor preset. A two-axis human study shows that raters prefer the vivid end for matching the reference but the default as a standalone photo, a trade-off the knob spans without retraining.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-27T09:30:35Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Accepted to WACV 2027</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Beomjin Ahn</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.35959v1</id>
    <title>AgentBaton: Efficient Model Collaboration in Agentic Tasks</title>
    <updated>2026-10-25T06:15:26Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.35959v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.35959v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Solving long-horizon agentic tasks requires language agents to interact with their environments over multiple steps, and reliable performance often comes from the expensive capable models available through cloud providers. Meanwhile, alternative efficient models with inferior performance but a much lower cost are emerging, including lower-tier cloud models and smaller open-weights models deployable on local hardware. This raises a natural question that remains less explored than traditional query-level routing: can capable and efficient models collaborate within a single long-horizon agentic task to substantially reduce cost while preserving task accuracy? In this paper, we systematically investigate key design choices for such collaboration through model handoffs, where one model takes over from another, focusing on handoff direction and context transfer. We find that efficient-to-capable handoffs generally underperform capable-only execution with limited or even no cost reduction, whereas capable-to-efficient handoffs at suitable stages of task progress largely preserve or even improve accuracy while substantially reducing cost. These findings suggest that a one-time, simple, well-timed handoff strategy is good enough for cost-effective agent collaboration, motivating AgentBaton, a lightweight detector that learns when to hand off from recurring patterns in task progress. Across 11 model pairs and two representative benchmarks, AgentBaton reduces API costs by 65.9% on average while largely preserving task accuracy, with accuracy gains of up to 8.8 percentage points over capable-only execution. Experiments across various reasoning-effort settings further show that AgentBaton extends the empirical accuracy-cost Pareto frontier.</summary>
    <category term="cs.MA" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-25T06:15:26Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.MA"/>
    <author>
      <name>Junlong Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jindong Jiang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yonggan Fu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jiarui Yao</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Hanrong Ye</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Wonmin Byeon</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Peter Belcak</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Hao Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Pavlo Molchanov</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37570v1</id>
    <title>LCM-Promo: An LLM-Based Uplift Model for Promotion Allocation</title>
    <updated>2026-10-27T17:15:15Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37570v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.37570v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We present LCM-Promo, an LLM-based uplift model for promotion allocation. LCM-Promo is a task-specific Large Commerce Model (LCM): a finetuned LLM trained on transactional commerce data to predict user intent and promotion response. It learns monotonic promotion-response curves from production logs by interpreting single-token outputs as conversion-threshold probabilities, while conditioning on session context such as day and delivery shift. In a production A/B test on churn reactivation, LCM-Promo increased conversion by 1% and reduced promotion cost by 6% relative to the Segment-EMA production baseline.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.IR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-27T17:15:15Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>4 pages, 2 tables. Accepted at ECML PKDD 2026 (Industrial Track)</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <arxiv:journal_ref>In: Machine Learning and Knowledge Discovery in Databases. Applied Data Science Track, Demo Track and Industrial Track. ECML PKDD 2026. Lecture Notes in Computer Science (LNAI), vol 16950, pp. 450-453. Springer, Cham (2026)</arxiv:journal_ref>
    <author>
      <name>Nesta Midavaine</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Floris Fok</name>
    </author>
