  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2512.23818v3</id>
    <title>Energy-Tweedie: Score meets Score, Energy meets Energy</title>
    <updated>2026-11-07T12:03:15Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.23818v3" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.23818v3" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Denoising and score estimation are classically linked through Tweedie's formula, which relates the posterior mean under Gaussian noise to the Stein score of the noisy marginal. In this work, we extend this perspective beyond Gaussian noise to a broad class of Gibbs (energy-based) noise distributions, with the generalized Gaussian family as an example. We derive the Energy-Tweedie identity: when the denoising posterior is viewed through the lens of scoring rules, the path derivative of a kernel scoring rule defined by the noise potential recovers the Stein score of the noisy marginal. Thus, the familiar correspondence between Gaussian noise, posterior means, squared loss, and Tweedie's formula is lifted to a distributional correspondence between Gibbs noise distributions, full posterior laws, kernel scoring rules, and the Energy-Tweedie identity, which gives a Tweedie-style relation for each noise potential. Among its consequences, this identity gives a posterior-samples-to-score route to score estimation, yields a principled criterion for estimating unknown noise parameters, and enables diffusion-style sampling along user-chosen paths through the noise-parameter space, supplying the score-based perspective on recent generative methods trained with scoring rules.</summary>
    <category term="stat.ML" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2025-12-29T19:28:50Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Code repository: https://github.com/andleb/et. NeurIPS 2026 Camera-ready, 59 pages, 16 figures</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="stat.ML"/>
    <author>
      <name>Andrej Leban</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10690v1</id>
    <title>JoVox: Joint Hierarchical Flow Matching for Lightweight Voxel Generation</title>
    <updated>2026-11-07T12:21:25Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10690v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10690v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Generating high-fidelity voxels while keeping their lightweight nature remains an open problem in computer vision. Current generators are either high-fidelity but computationally heavy, or lightweight but of lower fidelity. Hierarchical methods keep the lightweight nature of voxels. However, their coarse levels, which decide where all finer cells can appear, are generated with the least information about the shape and are never revisited, so they are often plausible but wrong. Our key insight is to let finer cells take part in deciding coarse cells. To this end, we propose JoVox, a discrete flow matching framework that learns the joint distribution of all levels of the voxel hierarchy, instead of a separate distribution for each level, so that coarse cells can be predicted with finer cells already generated nearby. Within this framework, we further introduce a two-level prediction, which outputs the cells two levels finer at once instead of one level finer, halving the number of levels that the network processes and yielding voxels of higher fidelity. We also introduce an unmasking strategy that revisits early decisions once finer cells are generated and corrects inconsistencies in masked diffusion models. Our framework outperforms the baselines on both unconditional and image-conditioned voxel generation. Compared with the current state-of-the-art TRELLIS.2, we achieve 8x higher training throughput, 1/15 of the FLOPs in a forward pass, train on 4 vs. 32 GPUs, and obtain higher quality with an 11% lower Chamfer distance.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.GT" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-07T12:21:25Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Xiangjun Tang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jianqi Chen</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Peter Wonka</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12063v1</id>
    <title>Toward Verifiable Auto-Research for Data Systems</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T08:39:16Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12063v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12063v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>AI auto-research is poised to forge bespoke data systems, from key-value stores and indexes to query engines, each engineered for a specific workload and hardware platform. Yet this promise rests on fragile ground. An agent optimizes what it can measure, not necessarily what users need. Natural-language requirements leave ample room for specification gaming, producing systems that blaze through benchmarks while silently losing updates or violating consistency. Worse, each research iteration consumes tokens, while validating a candidate under real concurrency, memory pressure, and failure conditions can stretch the entire research process into weeks.
  In this paper, we envision a more cost-efficient and verifiable approach to auto-research. Precise and composable specifications anchor the search, lightweight performance critics prune costly dead ends, and falsification and deductive verification make correctness an integral part of the process rather than an afterthought. By treating correctness, performance, research time, and token cost as equally important objectives, we aim to transform AI-generated data systems from plausible artifacts into trustworthy, production-grade systems whose behavior can be understood, audited, and ultimately relied upon.</summary>
    <category term="cs.DB" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T08:39:16Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.DB"/>
    <author>
      <name>Kaiwen Zheng</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Srijan Srivastava</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zheng Yan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xinpeng Zhao</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Shaohua Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Chieh-Jan Mike Liang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Tianyu Chen</name>
    </author>
